    "notes": "Q828143/relation1767601 identifies the archaeological precinct. Thirty standard PMTiles records lying within its boundary supply eight wall strips/cores and twenty-two isolated fragments; the boundary itself is not extruded. Geometry retains raw east/south coordinates at heading0, with internal map-axis rotation32.14degrees. Two ordinary pictures guide eroded concrete/masonry, shallow gated recesses and pale carved remnants. Recess dimensions/position on the exposed western foundation are approximate visualizations, not surveyed entrance controls. The neighboring three-column Vespasian temple and later buildings are excluded. Reused pinned temple_of_portunus Rome PMTiles/Mapzen grid, sampled at this anchor. The long low remnants follow the native grade, with buried retaining bases. The shallow recesses have closed floors just above the sampled grade and opaque backs; they do not expose an underground interior. Preserve all native ground and water with no cutout. Exact archaeological pavement and hillside retaining levels are not surveyed. All 1,904 independent in-tile buildings remain unchanged, including Tabularium, Vespasian, the church and neighboring Forum structures. Thirty authored map records are replaced to boundary remnants below 1e-7m². Native ground and original Tiber/water geometry remain unchanged. Failed loading restores all 1,934 original records; eviction releases geometry. The source photographs identify the exposed foundation forms; exact gate/recess locations and relative archaeological levels remain approximate."
  },
  "fidelityReview": {
    "target": "medium-fi",
    "status": "passed",
    "notes": "Accepted as a medium-fi present-day exposed ruin exterior and approximate standard-map fit. Exact mapped remnant footprints control the scattered arrangement. Wall heights, erosion, mixed masonry, stone inventory, moulded pieces and shallow arched/flat-lintel recess positions are picture-led visualization estimates. Native ground stays visible in the gaps and across the unbuilt precinct. Recesses have opaque backs and closed floors and do not expose an underground interior. Independent neighboring monuments are procedural context, with some retained occlusion/shadows. No lost roof, colonnade, tall cella, exact carving inventory, reconstructed ancient temple or navigable interior is claimed. The ordinary pictures date from 2018, so exact current vegetation and surface damage are not certified."
